# ansible-lab/.gitignore
# Files and directories Git should never track.
# These fall into two categories:
#   1. Secrets — must never reach a remote repository
#   2. Generated files — can always be recreated, no value in tracking them

# ── Ansible runtime files ─────────────────────────────────────────
*.retry
# Ansible writes a .retry file listing failed hosts when a playbook
# fails. It is a convenience file for re-running against failed hosts
# only — it has no value in version history.

.vault_pass
vault_pass.txt
# If you use ansible-vault to encrypt secrets, the vault password
# file must NEVER be committed. This is the equivalent of committing
# your encryption key alongside your encrypted files.

# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.sops.yml
# SOPS-encrypted files. If you add SOPS to this project later,
# the encrypted files are safe to commit (they are ciphertext),
# but this line serves as a reminder to be explicit about it.
# Remove this line only when you have SOPS properly configured.

*secret*
*password*
*credentials*
# Catch-all for any accidentally created files with these names.

# ── SSH keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa
id_ed25519
# Private key files. A private key committed to a repo — even a
# private repo — is compromised and must be rotated immediately.

# ── Editor and OS junk ───────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
# Editor temp files and OS metadata. These vary per person and
# have no meaning in the project — tracking them causes noisy diffs.

# ── Python ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.python-version
venv/
.venv/
# Python bytecode and virtual environment directories.
# These are always machine-specific and can be recreated.

# ── Logs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
# Log files grow unboundedly and contain runtime data, not config.
# Track the config that generates logs, not the logs themselves.
